.TH NA_AUDIO_SOURCE 1 "2026-09-02" "naudio" "naudio manual"
.SH NAME
na_audio_source \- broadcast an audio input or a test tone as a naudio stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B na_audio_source
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-capture\-id
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-playback\-id
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-transport
.IR tcp | udp ]
.RB [ \-\-reliability
.IR lan | wan ]
.RB [ \-\-rate
.IR HZ ]
.RB [ \-\-channels
.IR 1 | 2 ]
.RB [ \-\-test\-tone ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-clients
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-seconds
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR LABEL ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-discovery ]
.RB [ \-\-discovery\-port
.IR N ]
.br
.B na_audio_source \-\-list\-devices
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B na_audio_source
is the simplest way to put audio on the network with naudio: it starts a
streaming server and broadcasts either a local audio input (a microphone,
line-in, or any capture device) or \(em with
.B \-\-test\-tone
\(em a built-in tone that needs no audio hardware at all.
Anything that speaks the naudio protocol can listen:
.BR na_c_play_to_speakers (1)
plays the stream on another machine's speakers,
.BR na_wav_tap (1)
records it to a WAV file.
.PP
With
.B \-\-playback\-id
the server also plays the clients' transmit audio on a local output device.
naudio arbitrates one transmit channel among its clients; whichever client
holds it is heard on that device \(em the radio's transmit audio input at a
station, or the speakers on a desk.
.PP
With
.B \-\-test\-tone
it is the out-of-the-box demo and the first thing to run on a new install:
start it on one machine, point the player at that machine from anywhere on
the network, and you hear the tone \(em proof the whole path works before
any real audio source is involved.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
port to listen on; 0 lets the OS pick one (default 4533).
.TP
.BI \-\-capture\-id " N"
audio input device to broadcast, by the id shown by
.B \-\-list\-devices
(default: the first capture device).
.TP
.BI \-\-playback\-id " N"
output device to also play the clients' transmit audio on, by the id shown in
the playback list of
.B \-\-list\-devices
(default: transmit audio is not played).
Capture mode only \(em the test tone opens no device.
The device is opened at the server's rate and channel count, like the capture
device, and an id that no playback-capable device carries is refused before the
server starts.
.TP
.BI \-\-transport " tcp\fR|\fPudp"
network transport (default tcp).
Connecting clients must use the same one.
.TP
.BI \-\-reliability " lan\fR|\fPwan"
loss-recovery profile for UDP (forward error correction, reordering,
jitter buffering).
UDP requires one \(em plain UDP with no recovery is refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " HZ"
sample rate, 8000..192000 and divisible by 50 (default 48000).
naudio never resamples: in capture mode the device must support the
requested rate or the start fails.
Lower rates cost proportionally less network bandwidth \(em 12000\ Hz mono
is 192\ kbps against the 1536\ kbps default.
.TP
.BI \-\-channels " N"
1 (mono) or 2 (stereo, the default); server-wide, like
.BR \-\-rate .
.TP
.B \-\-test\-tone
broadcast a deterministic built-in tone instead of a capture device.
Needs no audio hardware.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-clients " N"
maximum simultaneous listeners (default 4).
.TP
.BI \-\-seconds " N"
run time in seconds; 0 runs until Ctrl-C (default 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-name " LABEL"
operator label reported in discovery replies, e.g. "shack" (default: none). Truncated to
255 bytes on the wire. Has no effect with
.BR \-\-no\-discovery .
.TP
.BI \-\-discovery\-port " N"
the rendezvous port this server also listens on for
.B DISCOVER
probes (default 4533; 0 runs no rendezvous listener). A client that knows only this number
finds the server whatever port it serves audio on, because the reply carries the service
port. This is also what makes a
.B TCP
server discoverable at all \(em TCP has no datagram path to an unknown sender, and TCP and
UDP port spaces are independent, so a TCP server on 4533 still gets UDP 4533 here. Binding
it is best effort: where several servers share a host the first to start wins it, and the
others are found only by a probe aimed at their own port.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-discovery
do not answer
.B DISCOVER
probes. Servers answer by default (wire spec 1.4, \[u00A7]6.8): a probe creates no
connection, costs no slot against
.BR \-\-max\-clients ,
and starts no stream \(em but answering does tell anyone on the local segment that this
server exists, what audio format it serves and how full it is. This is a discoverability
switch, not access control: a silent server still accepts connections from anyone who
already knows its address.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-devices
print the ids of capture-capable devices, then of playback-capable ones, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
print usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Serve the test tone (no audio hardware needed):
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_audio_source \-\-test\-tone
.RE
.fi
.PP
Broadcast the first microphone on the machine over UDP with loss recovery:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_audio_source \-\-transport udp \-\-reliability wan
.RE
.fi
.PP
Serve a microphone and play the clients' transmit audio on the speakers (both
ids from
.BR \-\-list\-devices ):
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_audio_source \-\-capture\-id 1 \-\-playback\-id 2
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR na_c_play_to_speakers (1),
.BR na_wav_tap (1),
.BR na_audio_daemon (1)
